Here Be Spoilers!

















Excerpts from netnews.rec.games.hack: 10-Mar-93 Adventurer's Guide to
Align.. by Robert DeLoura@noaapmel. 
>        -15                     Killing any tame creature.

I guess that offing kitty at the beginning of the game isn't such a
great idea.  My first action is to eliminate the little hairball (dog or
cat, I don't care), since I hate competing for corpses and I just don't
like to have to keep track of them.  I might rethink this, though.

>         +MAXALIGN/4             Killing a priest of Moloch. (very good)

Seriously?  Geez, I didn't know this.  I guess when you wipe out all
those priests and priestesses on Moloch's level, your alignment is going
to come out smelling like a rose.  Which makes me wonder...how is it
possible that somebody would NOT end up with a guardian angel?

On another note, does anybody know what effect it has to anger other
gods?  When I was playing a Valkyrie once, I sacrificed on a Lawful
altar (I was neutral) and the priest got mad.  So I chopped his head
off, like any self respecting Valkyrie with the Vorpal Blade would have
done.  At this point, Tyr got mad.  Later in the game, an Angel of Tyr
showed up and danced with me for a while before I chopped it down to
size (with a pick axe, but that is another story).  The Angel kept
taunting me, saying it was going to kill me in the name of Tyr and
whatnot.  Was this because I killed one of Tyr's priests or was it just
general, non-personal animosity.  Are there any game effects I should
care about with angering a non-coaligned god?

> Choking on your food is -1 to alignment if you're a Lawful Knight (big deal,
> if you choke on your food you're likely to be dead.)

That's pretty dopey.  What is this, gluttony?

> If you are at least stridently aligned, you'll receive a pet Guardian Angel
> when you reach the Astral Plane.

Incidentally, DON'T put on a Ring of Conflict if you get a Guardian
Angel, unless you want to anger it and make it attack you.  I did this,
and was forced to kill an Angel of my own god, thus probably NOT scoring
brownie points with Crom.  'Course, I sacrificed the Amulet to him
shortly thereafter, so he couldn't have been that upset, but still.

While I'm babbling, anybody manage to sacrifice the bodies of
Pestilence, Death, Taxes or the other assorted nasties on the Astral
Plane?  What happens?

